meta {
  name: Create Account (Sender)
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/account
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

script:pre-request {
  bru.setVar("senderEmail", `sender.${Date.now()}@example.com`);
}

body:json {
  {
    "email": "{{senderEmail}}",
    "password": "SuperSecret123!"
  }
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.body && res.body.result && res.body.result.accountId) {
    bru.setEnvVar("senderAccountId", res.body.result.accountId);
    bru.setEnvVar("accountId", res.body.result.accountId);
  }
}

docs {
  Signup endpoint. Deliberately unauthenticated — a brand-new caller has no
  bearer token yet, so it can't sit behind authMiddleware like Wallet and
  Remittance do.

  Provisions two aggregates from one call: a User (identity/credentials —
  email + hashed password) and an Account (the financial/ledger
  relationship Wallet/Remittance/KycProfile reference by id). The response
  is Account-shaped (accountId, email, status, createdAt) — it does not
  return userId; if you need that id, read it from Auth / Login's response
  instead, which resolves the User behind the email/password and returns
  both userId and accountId.

  On success, stores the returned accountId in the environment variable
  `senderAccountId` (and also in `accountId`, used by the Wallet requests
  below) so you can chain straight into "Open Wallet" and "Send Remittance"
  without copy-pasting IDs by hand.

  Run Auth / Login next — it reuses the `senderEmail` runtime variable this
  request sets, plus the same fixed password, to mint a real bearer token
  (POST /login, backed by the User domain — see domain/user/entities/user.ts).
}
